WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.385
A responsible assistant needs clear limits.

00:00:03.385 --> 00:00:08.253
A written instruction can help it explain a policy to a customer.

00:00:08.253 --> 00:00:15.901
If a mistake could move money or change someone’s account, the application needs a way to enforce the limit too.

00:00:15.901 --> 00:00:22.452
A guardrail is a check the program applies regardless of what the assistant asks for.

00:00:22.452 --> 00:00:28.204
Think of a till that requires a supervisor’s approval for a large refund.

00:00:28.204 --> 00:00:34.182
The cashier cannot remove that requirement just by describing the refund differently.

00:00:34.182 --> 00:00:36.683
Match each permission to the task.

00:00:36.683 --> 00:00:41.291
Reading an order and issuing money have different consequences.

00:00:41.291 --> 00:00:48.771
Keep customer information private, and give the customer a clear explanation when a person must take over.

00:00:48.771 --> 00:00:55.749
Since Module 2, Scout's system prompt has said that refunds over two hundred dollars need a person.

00:00:55.749 --> 00:01:01.212
A rule in the prompt is a request, and the model follows it most of the time.

00:01:01.212 --> 00:01:05.526
Most of the time is not good enough when the action moves money.

00:01:05.526 --> 00:01:11.287
A guardrail is a check in your own code that runs whatever the model decides.

00:01:11.287 --> 00:01:17.704
If the model is confused, or tricked, the guardrail still holds, and it leaves a record.

00:01:17.704 --> 00:01:21.590
Owen asks Orbit for three hundred and ten dollars back.

00:01:21.590 --> 00:01:25.306
Scout can look up his order to understand the request.

00:01:25.306 --> 00:01:28.012
Reading that record changes nothing.

00:01:28.012 --> 00:01:38.369
Sending money would change the shop’s accounts, so the application checks Orbit’s example policy before it allows a refund to go any further.

00:01:38.369 --> 00:01:44.674
Not every tool call deserves the same caution, so sort actions by what they could break.

00:01:44.674 --> 00:01:50.597
A permitted lookup reads an order after the shop has checked the customer's access to it.

00:01:50.597 --> 00:01:57.042
A refund up to two hundred dollars moves money, so Scout may do it, and your code logs it.

00:01:57.042 --> 00:02:03.166
A refund over two hundred dollars is paused until a person on Leo's team approves it.

00:02:03.166 --> 00:02:10.634
Anything that cannot be undone, like closing a customer's account, is simply not one of Scout's tools.

00:02:10.634 --> 00:02:17.352
For every tool, ask: what is the worst case if this call is wrong, and can we undo it?

00:02:17.352 --> 00:02:22.984
The policy requires a person to approve a refund over two hundred dollars.

00:02:22.984 --> 00:02:26.180
Compare three hundred and ten with two hundred.

00:02:26.180 --> 00:02:29.086
The request stops at the approval step.

00:02:29.086 --> 00:02:32.706
Scout can explain that the team needs to review it.

00:02:32.706 --> 00:02:38.722
A promise that the money has already been sent would misdescribe the actual state.

00:02:38.722 --> 00:02:42.521
Put two pretend refunds beside the shop's rule.

00:02:42.521 --> 00:02:45.938
The first is exactly two hundred dollars.

00:02:45.938 --> 00:02:48.248
Read the word over carefully.

00:02:48.248 --> 00:02:56.240
Under Orbit's example policy this particular approval threshold does not fire at exactly two hundred.

00:02:56.240 --> 00:03:00.606
Change only the amount to two hundred dollars and one cent.

00:03:00.606 --> 00:03:03.546
The request now crosses the threshold.

00:03:03.546 --> 00:03:12.774
A useful test checks whether the system actually pauses before moving money, even if the assistant writes a confident reply.

00:03:12.774 --> 00:03:16.693
Look at the payment record and the message side by side.

00:03:16.693 --> 00:03:22.092
If the record says pending review, the customer should hear that review is pending.

00:03:22.092 --> 00:03:24.937
A polite instruction helps the wording.

00:03:24.937 --> 00:03:29.194
The payment check enforces what can actually happen.

00:03:29.194 --> 00:03:34.808
Suppose Owen adds a sentence saying the usual approval is unnecessary.

00:03:34.808 --> 00:03:39.576
It is still a customer message, not authorization from the shop.

00:03:39.576 --> 00:03:46.245
Test the result by checking whether money moved and whether an approval request was recorded.

00:03:46.245 --> 00:03:53.211
Those records tell you whether the guardrail worked, even if the assistant’s words sounded convincing.
